The `@QueryHints` annotation on a repository method passes optimization hints to the JPA provider (Hibernate). These hints are applied to the specific Query object created for that method, not to the entire transaction.

```java
// ═══ readOnly hint — skip dirty checking for THIS query ═══
@QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.readOnly", value = "true"))
List<Trade> findByStatus(String status);
// Entities loaded by THIS method: no snapshot stored, no dirty checking at flush.
// Other queries in the SAME transaction still have dirty checking.

// ═══ cacheable hint — use second-level query cache ═══
@QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.cacheable", value = "true"))
List<Trade> findBySymbol(String symbol);
// First call: SQL executes, result cached in L2 query cache.
// Next identical call: returns cached result WITHOUT hitting DB!
// Requires: hibernate.cache.use_query_cache=true in configuration.

// ═══ fetchSize hint — rows per JDBC round-trip ═══
@QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.fetchSize", value = "100"))
List<Trade> findByAccountId(String accountId);
// JDBC driver fetches 100 rows per network round-trip instead of default (10-50).
// For 10,000 rows: default fetchSize=10 → 1000 round-trips. fetchSize=100 → 100 round-trips.

// ═══ timeout hint — cancel slow queries ═══
@QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "jakarta.persistence.query.timeout", value = "5000"))
List<Trade> findByDateRange(LocalDate from, LocalDate to);
// If query takes > 5000ms → QueryTimeoutException. Prevents connection pool exhaustion.

// ═══ MULTIPLE hints on one method ═══
@QueryHints({
    @QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.readOnly", value = "true"),
    @QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.fetchSize", value = "200"),
    @QueryHint(name = "jakarta.persistence.query.timeout", value = "10000")
})
List<Trade> findLargeDataset(Pageable pageable);
```

The key difference between `@QueryHints(readOnly)` and `@Transactional(readOnly=true)`:

```java
@Transactional  // NOT readOnly — this transaction may write
public void processAndReport(String accountId) {

    // Query 1: read-only lookup (hint: skip dirty checking for THIS query)
    List<Trade> trades = tradeRepo.findByStatus("SETTLED");
    // @QueryHints(readOnly=true) on findByStatus → these Trade entities are read-only
    // Hibernate does NOT store snapshots for these entities. Saves memory.

    // Query 2: writable lookup (no hint — normal dirty checking)
    Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
    account.setBalance(newBalance);
    // This Account entity IS dirty-checked (no readOnly hint on findById).
    // At flush: UPDATE accounts SET balance=... generated.

    // If @Transactional(readOnly=true) were used instead:
    // BOTH queries would be read-only — account.setBalance() change would be SILENTLY IGNORED!
}
```

